{"id": "763234c1-bd14-473a-97d1-478f8bc1403e", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm working out how EstimatedTPMQuotaUsage is calculated for Bedrock, specifically how output tokens get multiplied by a burndown factor (5x for Sonnet-class models like Sonnet 4) alongside input and cache-write tokens to determine total quota consumption.

Computing input plus 5x output gives 66.6M, but the observed quota usage of 20.14M is actually lower than input+output (36.59M) alone, which can't be right under any burndown rate—so something about the metric math itself is flawed. I suspect the issue is the CloudWatch statistic: using Maximum over 60-second periods only captures the single largest per-request value in that minute, not the total volume, so summing Maximums drastically undercounts versus using Sum. That would explain the discrepancy rather than implying there's no burndown at all. I should verify the actual burndown rate for this model via a documentation check rather than relying purely on training data.

